<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yazoo River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yazoo River flows about 300 km through western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, to join the Mississippi River just north of Vicksburg. In 1764 the latitude of its mouth was used to fix the northern boundary of British West <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>. Its name was later attached to the Yazoo land scandal of 1795, a fraudulent sale of western lands by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> legislature.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yazoo-river.jpg" alt="Yazoo River" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alfred Dulaney, U.S. Army Corps of Engineers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Yazoo_River_control_structure.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
